.TH SYNC_OPERATOR_FIELD 1 "2026-08-26" "rptsched-cleanup" "rptsched-cleanup Manual"
.SH NAME
sync_operator_field.py \- correct the operator field inside .set files to match the schedlist owner
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sync_operator_field.py
.B \-\-data\-dir
.I DIR
.B \-\-quarantine\-dir
.I DIR
[\fB\-\-execute\fR | \fB\-\-restore\fR \fIRUN_DIR\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sync_operator_field.py
finds \fI<id>.set\fR files whose internal \fBoperator\fR field disagrees
with the \fBowner\fR recorded for that id in \fIschedlist\fR, and rewrites
the \fB.set\fR file in place to match.

Each \fI<id>.set\fR file contains a pipe\-delimited line beginning
\fBoperator|\fR, whose second\-to\-last field holds the Symphony operator
id that field is meant to agree with the owning template's
\fBowner\fR field in \fIschedlist\fR (field index 6). Drift between the
two can accumulate for reasons outside this tool's scope; this tool
detects and fixes it, one field, one file, at a time.

This is field\-level surgery, not a quarantine\-and\-remove operation
like \fBremove_orphans.py\fR(1) or \fBremove_stale_templates.py\fR(1):
no files are moved out of \fB\-\-data\-dir\fR. \fB\-\-quarantine\-dir\fR
is used only to hold the run's \fImanifest.csv\fR, which records enough
to reverse every field edit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-data\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Path to the \fIrptsched\fR data directory to scan and modify. Required in
every mode, including \fB\-\-restore\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-quarantine\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Path to the directory that holds run subfolders (and their
\fImanifest.csv\fR files). Created if it does not already exist. Required
in every mode.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Perform the field rewrites. Without this flag the script runs in
dry\-run mode and writes nothing.
.TP
.BR \-\-restore " " \fIRUN_DIR\fR
Reverse a previous \fB\-\-execute\fR run's field edits using
\fIRUN_DIR\fR/manifest.csv. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-execute\fR.
.SH MODES
.SS Dry run (default)
For every id present in \fIschedlist\fR, the script looks for a matching
\fI<id>.set\fR file and extracts its \fBoperator\fR field:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
If the \fB.set\fR file is missing, or has no \fBoperator|\fR line, the id
is skipped with a \fBWARNING\fR on stderr (reason given) and excluded
from the mismatch count entirely \- it is not something this tool can
fix.
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise, if the extracted operator differs from the \fIschedlist\fR
owner, it is reported as a mismatch: \fBid: old_operator \-> new_operator\fR.
.RE
.IP
Nothing on disk is touched in dry\-run mode.
.SS Execute
With \fB\-\-execute\fR, the script re\-finds mismatches (skips are
reported the same way as dry run) and, if any exist, creates
\fB\-\-quarantine\-dir\fR/operators_\fITIMESTAMP\fR, then for each
mismatched id in id order:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Rewrites the \fB.set\fR file's \fBoperator|\fR line in place \- via a
temp file plus atomic rename, preserving the original file's permissions
and mtime metadata \- setting the operator field to the \fIschedlist\fR
owner value.
.IP 2. 4
Appends a row to the run's \fImanifest.csv\fR (id, old_operator,
new_operator).
.RE
.IP
If zero mismatches are found, the script prints a message and exits 0
without creating a run subfolder. If a rewrite fails partway through, the
ids already corrected keep their manifest row (so they're all
individually restorable), a partial \fImanifest.csv\fR is written, and
the script exits non\-zero.
.SS Restore
With \fB\-\-restore\fR \fIRUN_DIR\fR, the script reads
\fIRUN_DIR\fR/manifest.csv and, for each row, reads the id's
\fBcurrent\fR operator value and compares it against the row's recorded
\fBold_operator\fR and \fBnew_operator\fR:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Current equals \fBold_operator\fR \(em already restored (by an earlier
attempt, or never actually changed); skipped.
.IP \(bu 2
Current equals \fBnew_operator\fR \(em this is the normal case; the field
is rewritten back to \fBold_operator\fR and counted as restored.
.IP \(bu 2
Current matches neither \(em something else modified the field since
this tool last touched it. The script prints an error naming the id and
all three values, and aborts immediately with a non\-zero exit rather
than guessing which value is correct.
.RE
.IP
This makes restore idempotent and resumable in the same spirit as
\fBremove_orphans.py\fR(1)'s file\-restore: safe to re\-run on the same
\fIRUN_DIR\fR, and it stops rather than overwrites when something looks
wrong.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success \- including a clean dry run, an execute run that found zero
mismatches, or a restore that skipped everything because it was already
complete.
.TP
.B 1
A manifest could not be read (wrong file / wrong tool's run directory), a
field rewrite failed, or a restore hit an id whose current value matched
neither the recorded old nor new value.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <data\-dir>/schedlist
Read only \- supplies the \fBowner\fR value each \fB.set\fR file's
\fBoperator\fR field is compared and synced against. Never modified by
this tool.
.TP
.I <data\-dir>/<id>.set
Rewritten in place (atomically) for each mismatched id, both by
\fB\-\-execute\fR (old operator \-> new) and by \fB\-\-restore\fR
(new operator \-> old, when applicable).
.TP
.I <quarantine\-dir>/operators_<timestamp>/manifest.csv
Written by \fB\-\-execute\fR; read by \fB\-\-restore\fR. Columns: id,
old_operator, new_operator. Distinct schema from the
\fBremove_orphans.py\fR(1) / \fBremove_stale_templates.py\fR(1) manifest
\- the two are not interchangeable, and \fB\-\-restore\fR rejects a
manifest with the wrong columns rather than misinterpreting it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Preview operator/owner mismatches:
.B sync_operator_field.py \-\-data\-dir ./rptsched \-\-quarantine\-dir ./quarantine
.TP
Apply the corrections:
.B sync_operator_field.py \-\-data\-dir ./rptsched \-\-quarantine\-dir ./quarantine \-\-execute
.TP
Undo a specific run:
.B sync_operator_field.py \-\-data\-dir ./rptsched \-\-quarantine\-dir ./quarantine \-\-restore ./quarantine/operators_20260826_140501
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR remove_orphans.py (1),
.BR remove_stale_templates.py (1)
.SH CAVEATS
Requires Python 3.6.8, standard library only.

Unlike the other two tools, nothing is ever moved out of
\fB\-\-data\-dir\fR \- there is no quarantined copy of a \fB.set\fR file
to fall back on, only the manifest's record of the old and new field
values. If a \fB.set\fR file is edited by something else between
\fB\-\-execute\fR and \fB\-\-restore\fR, restore for that id aborts
rather than clobbering the unexpected value, leaving it for the operator
to check that id and resolve the mismatch by hand.
